(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-exam-style-quiz"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 — Exam-Style Review Quiz","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A Gram-positive organism is isolated from a wound. Reasoning forward from what its wall is built like, what is it vulnerable to and what will it shrug off?","choices":["Vulnerable to detergents alone; protected by lipopolysaccharide","Vulnerable to polymyxin; protected by its thick peptidoglycan from lysozyme","Vulnerable to nothing that acts on the wall; protected by its periplasmic space","Vulnerable to lysozyme and penicillin; it has no outer membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One thick exposed peptidoglycan layer is both the enzyme's substrate and the drug's target, and with no outer membrane there is no permeability barrier to overcome. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 24-29"},{"q":"Which wall feature explains why Gram-negative organisms resist many agents that kill Gram-positive ones, and what does that same feature release when the cell dies?","choices":["A waxy mycolic acid layer, which releases cord factor","A thick peptidoglycan layer, which releases teichoic acid","An outer membrane, which releases lipid A as endotoxin","A capsule, which releases exotoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The outer membrane excludes large and hydrophobic molecules, and its lipopolysaccharide carries lipid A, the toxic portion freed when the cell lyses. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 26-29"},{"q":"Mycoplasmas are singled out among bacteria. What makes them unusual, and what two consequences follow directly from it?","choices":["They lack a cell wall, so they cannot be Gram stained and penicillins do not work","They lack ribosomes, so they cannot make protein and need a host cell","They lack deoxyribonucleic acid, so they need a host cell","They have two cell walls, so they stain unpredictably and resist lysozyme"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no peptidoglycan there is nothing to retain crystal violet and nothing for a cell-wall-active drug to attack, so both the stain and the penicillins are useless. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 14-18"},{"q":"Which structure lets a bacterium survive heat, drying and disinfectants for years, and which lets it resist being engulfed by a phagocyte?","choices":["The plasmid for survival; the fimbria for resisting phagocytosis","The capsule for survival; the endospore for resisting phagocytosis","The flagellum for survival; the pilus for resisting phagocytosis","The endospore for survival; the capsule for resisting phagocytosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The endospore is a dormant dehydrated form that withstands extremes, while the capsule's slippery polysaccharide prevents a phagocyte gripping the cell. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 30-36"},{"q":"Blood agar is both enriched and differential. What does each of those words buy you, and what are the three results you read off it?","choices":["Enriched suppresses unwanted growth, differential supplies nutrients; acid, gas and no change","Enriched supplies extra nutrients, differential shows visible differences; gamma, alpha and beta haemolysis","Enriched selects one species, differential identifies it; coagulase, catalase and oxidase results","Enriched raises the incubation temperature, differential lowers the pH; growth, no growth and partial growth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The added blood feeds fastidious organisms and simultaneously acts as the indicator, since each organism digests haemoglobin to a different degree: none, partial, or complete. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 43-44"},{"q":"Mannitol salt agar is selective and differential at once. What is each property doing?","choices":["Both properties select, one for salt tolerance and one for sugar tolerance","The mannitol selects fermenters; the salt differentiates by colony colour","The salt lyses red blood cells; the mannitol supplies iron","The salt selects salt-tolerant organisms; mannitol then differentiates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only halotolerant organisms such as staphylococci grow at that salt concentration, and among those the pH indicator then separates the mannitol fermenters. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 43-44"},{"q":"Bacterial growth means an increase in what, by what process, and why does that make the rise exponential rather than steady?","choices":["Increase in colony diameter, by migration, because motile cells spread outward","Increase in cell SIZE, by binary fission, because each cell grows at a constant rate","Increase in cell number, by budding, because daughter cells stay attached","Increase in cell NUMBER, by binary fission, because each division doubles the population"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cells do not enlarge; each one splits into two, so the population goes 2, 4, 8, 16 rather than climbing by a fixed amount each generation. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 37-40"},{"q":"During which phase of the growth curve are bacteria most vulnerable to control methods, and why does that phase make them so?","choices":["Lag phase, because the cells have not yet adapted to the medium","Log phase, because rapidly dividing cells are actively building wall and nucleic acid","Stationary phase, because nutrients have run out and the cells are weakened","Death phase, because the population is already declining"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Agents that block cell wall synthesis or replication can only act where those processes are running, so the fastest-dividing population is the most exposed. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 37-40"},{"q":"In lysogeny the viral genome is incorporated into the host chromosome. What does the bacterium gain from that, and why does it matter clinically?","choices":["Loss of its plasmids, which removes resistance genes","Immediate lysis of neighbouring cells, which clears competing flora","New traits such as the ability to make a toxin, carried without any sign of infection","Permanent resistance to all further viral infection, with no other change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The integrated prophage is replicated along with the chromosome, so a harmless-looking organism can acquire and pass on a toxin gene, which is how diphtheria and cholera toxins arise. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 52-56"},{"q":"Animal virus replication differs from phage replication at entry, at the step that follows entry, and at release. What are those three differences?","choices":["The whole virion enters, it integrates immediately, and release is by lysis","Only the genome enters, uncoating follows, and release is by lysis","The whole virion enters, must be uncoated, and leaves by budding","Only the capsid enters, the genome stays outside, and release is by budding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A phage injects only its genome, so no uncoating is needed and the cell is lysed; an animal virus is engulfed entire, must shed its capsid, and can leave through the membrane acquiring an envelope. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A disinfectant reduces a population of 10,000,000 organisms to 10,000. How many decimal reductions is that, and what does the shape of a microbial death curve tell you?","choices":["Three, and death proceeds at a constant proportion rather than a constant number","Three, and death proceeds at a constant number of organisms per interval","Four, and death proceeds at a constant proportion of the survivors","One thousand, and the death rate accelerates as the population falls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each decimal reduction removes ninety per cent of whatever remains, so 10,000,000 to 10,000 is three tenfold steps, and the logarithmic curve reflects a fixed fraction dying per interval. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 62-68"},{"q":"A high mutation rate in a virus carries which practical consequence for the people it infects?","choices":["Antibiotic therapy becomes effective against it","The virus loses the ability to replicate inside host cells","Changed surface proteins can defeat existing immunity","Transmission between people stops, though animal spread continues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immunity recognises specific surface structures, so a virus that alters them is no longer matched by existing antibody, which is why some vaccines must be reformulated. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A drug has a toxic dose for half the population of 400 milligrams and an effective dose for half the population of 50 milligrams. What is its therapeutic index, and what does a larger value mean?","choices":["Three hundred and fifty, and a larger value means a safer drug","Eight, and a larger value means a more potent drug","One-eighth, and a smaller value means a safer drug","Eight, and a larger value means a safer drug"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The index is the toxic dose divided by the effective dose, so 400 over 50 is 8, and the wider that gap the more room there is between a dose that works and one that harms. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 18-20"},{"q":"An antibiotic course is followed by an infection that no longer responds to it. What has happened to the bacterial population?","choices":["The antibiotic caused the bacteria to develop resistance genes in response to it","Resistant organisms already present were selected for as the susceptible ones died","The bacteria learned to recognise the antibiotic and altered their behaviour","The host immune system destroyed the susceptible organisms and spared the rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The drug does not induce the trait; it removes the competition, so the few organisms that already carried resistance are left to multiply unopposed. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Which resistance mechanism destroys the antibiotic molecule itself, and which keeps the drug out or pushes it back out?","choices":["Efflux pumps destroy it; enzymatic inactivation excludes it","Enzymatic inactivation destroys it; permeability and efflux exclude it","Target modification destroys it; enzymatic inactivation excludes it","Biofilm formation destroys it; capsule production excludes it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactamase cleaves the drug so it can no longer bind, whereas permeability changes and pumps never let a useful concentration accumulate inside the cell. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Selective toxicity means finding a feature the invader has and we do not. Which target is genuinely absent from human cells, and which is present in both but different enough to exploit?","choices":["Deoxyribonucleic acid is absent from us; the mitochondrion is present in both","The ribosome is absent from us; the cell wall is present in both but differs in composition","The plasma membrane is absent from us; the nucleus is present in both","The peptidoglycan cell wall is absent from us; the ribosome is present in both but differs in size"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nothing in a human cell corresponds to peptidoglycan, so wall-active drugs are very safe, whereas the 70S bacterial ribosome differs enough from our 80S one to be targeted with more care. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 24-32"},{"q":"Which pairing correctly matches an antibiotic class to the step it blocks?","choices":["Sulfonamides block cell wall crosslinking; vancomycin blocks folate synthesis","Penicillins block protein synthesis; aminoglycosides block cell wall crosslinking","Penicillins block cell wall crosslinking; aminoglycosides block protein synthesis","Fluoroquinolones block membrane integrity; polymyxins block nucleic acid replication"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The beta-lactam ring inactivates the transpeptidase that crosslinks peptidoglycan, while the aminoglycosides bind the bacterial ribosome and halt translation. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 24-32"},{"q":"Why are there fewer drugs available against fungal and protozoal infections than against bacterial ones?","choices":["Those infections resolve without treatment, so few drugs were developed","Those pathogens reproduce too slowly for any drug to act on them","Those pathogens are prokaryotes with unusually tough cell walls","Those pathogens are eukaryotes, so they share far more structure with our own cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Selective toxicity depends on a difference to exploit, and a eukaryotic pathogen's cell structure and physiology closely resemble ours, so the margin for harming it without harming us is much narrower. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 12-16"},{"q":"What does drug clearance impose on the dosing schedule, and what happens if doses are spaced too far apart?","choices":["The level must stay above the minimal inhibitory concentration","Only the loading dose matters, since clearance does not affect the interval","Doses should be spaced as widely as the patient will tolerate to limit toxicity","The drug reaches its target instantly, so only the total daily amount matters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clearance removes the drug continuously, so once the level falls below the inhibitory concentration the suppressed population resumes multiplying before the next dose arrives. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 21-23"},{"q":"What separates a bacteriostatic from a bactericidal drug, and in which patient does that distinction become critical?","choices":["Static works on Gram-positive organisms and cidal on Gram-negative, which matters in sepsis","Static kills slowly and cidal kills quickly, which matters in the elderly patient","Static inhibits and needs host immunity; cidal kills, which matters if neutropenic","Static is oral and cidal is intravenous, which matters in the vomiting patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A bacteriostatic agent only halts replication and leaves clearance to the immune system, so a patient without functioning neutrophils needs a drug that kills outright. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 33-36"},{"q":"Which four problems accompany antimicrobial therapy, and what unites them?","choices":["Superinfection, direct toxicity, hypersensitivity and resistance — each a harm caused by treating","Cost, availability, storage and palatability — each a barrier to obtaining treatment","Superinfection, cost, resistance and slow onset — each a reason treatment fails","Allergy, nausea, headache and rash — each a minor adverse effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each is a different route by which treatment injures: killing protective flora, poisoning the host, provoking an immune reaction, or selecting organisms the drug can no longer touch. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 55-62"},{"q":"A patient develops profuse diarrhoea after a broad-spectrum antibiotic course. What has happened, and why did the antibiotic cause it?","choices":["Hypersensitivity, because the immune system reacted to the drug","Direct toxicity, because the drug damaged the intestinal lining","Superinfection, because the drug removed the flora holding it in check","Resistance, because the organism causing the original infection stopped responding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Resident flora occupy the niche and compete for nutrients, so wiping them out leaves room for a resistant organism such as Clostridioides difficile to overgrow. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 55-58"},{"q":"Which pairing of name to contribution is correct?","choices":["Florey observed penicillin; Fleming and Chain made it usable as a drug","Fleming observed penicillin; Florey and Chain made it usable as a drug","Ehrlich observed penicillin; Fleming made it usable as a drug","Chain observed penicillin; Ehrlich and Florey made it usable as a drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fleming noticed the inhibition around a contaminating mould, and it was Florey and Chain who purified the compound and demonstrated it could treat infection in patients. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 5-10"},{"q":"Resident flora protect us. By what mechanism, and which two sites carry essentially none?","choices":["They raise body temperature to inhibit invaders; the lungs and liver are sterile","They secrete antibodies against pathogens; the large intestine and skin are sterile","They digest all ingested pathogens; the stomach and mouth are sterile","They occupy the niche and prevent overgrowth; blood and spinal fluid are sterile"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Competition for space and nutrients is what keeps pathogens from establishing, and the internal compartments that no organism should reach are the blood and the cerebrospinal fluid. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"What separates a true pathogen from an opportunist, and what two circumstances let an opportunist cause disease?","choices":["A true pathogen is always Gram-negative; an opportunist needs a breach in the skin or a foreign body","A true pathogen sickens healthy people; an opportunist needs compromised defences or an unnatural site","A true pathogen is acquired in hospital; an opportunist is acquired in the community","A true pathogen produces exotoxin; an opportunist needs antibiotic exposure or advanced age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The true pathogen carries enough virulence to overcome intact defences, whereas an opportunist is usually harmless flora that causes disease only when immunity fails or it reaches somewhere it does not belong. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 18-24"},{"q":"How do endotoxin and exotoxin differ in origin, in when they are released, and in which organisms produce them?","choices":["Endotoxin is a protein secreted by living Gram-positive cells; exotoxin is released on lysis","Endotoxin is lipid A released on lysis by Gram-negative organisms; exotoxin is secreted by living cells","Endotoxin is released by fungi; exotoxin by bacteria, both while growing","Both are proteins, differing only in whether they act locally or systemically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endotoxin is a structural part of the Gram-negative outer membrane and only reaches the host when the cell breaks apart, whereas an exotoxin is a protein actively exported by a living bacterium. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Which virulence factor lets a bacterium attach to host tissue, and which lets it survive once a phagocyte has engulfed it?","choices":["Endotoxin for attachment; exotoxin for survival inside the phagocyte","Capsule for attachment; fimbriae for survival inside the phagocyte","Fimbriae for attachment; resisting digestion inside the phagocyte for survival","Flagella for attachment; spores for survival inside the phagocyte"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fimbriae are the adhesins that anchor the organism to a specific host receptor, and some organisms then avoid being killed by blocking fusion with the lysosome or withstanding its enzymes. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 30-38"},{"q":"A sign and a symptom are not the same thing. What separates them, and what is a sequela?","choices":["A sign is observed by the clinician and a symptom felt by the patient; a sequela is lasting damage afterwards","A sign is felt by the patient and a symptom observed by the clinician; a sequela is a relapse","A sign appears early and a symptom late; a sequela is a secondary infection","A sign is measurable and a symptom is not; a sequela is the incubation period"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The distinction is who perceives it, and a sequela is the long-term or permanent tissue or organ damage that remains once the acute illness has passed, such as blindness following gonococcal conjunctivitis. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 50-58"},{"q":"An infection confined to one area spreads through the bloodstream to distant organs. What is that called, and what is it called when a second organism takes hold after antibiotics disturb the flora?","choices":["Chronic infection; the second is a sequela","Superinfection; the second is a mixed infection","Secondary infection; the second is a latent infection","Systemic spread; the second is a superinfection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A localised infection becomes systemic when the agent breaks loose and disseminates, while a superinfection follows disruption of the natural microflora that had been suppressing the newcomer. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 50-58"},{"q":"Which body site carries the largest resident population, and which is sterile apart from a short segment?","choices":["The mouth carries the most; the large intestine is sterile","The stomach carries the most; the respiratory tract is sterile throughout","The skin carries the most; the gastrointestinal tract is sterile below the stomach","The large intestine carries the most; the urinary tract is sterile but for the distal urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The colon holds by far the densest microbial population in the body, while urine and the urinary tract above the distal urethra should contain no organisms at all. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"What does the infectious dose for half a population measure, and what does a LOW value indicate about the organism?","choices":["The dose killing half those infected; a low value means lower virulence","The dose infecting half those exposed; a low value means greater virulence","The proportion of exposed people who fall ill; a low value means high transmissibility","The time to symptom onset in half those infected; a low value means a short incubation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the number of organisms required to establish infection in fifty per cent of a population, so needing very few to do so marks an organism as highly virulent. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 26-30"},{"q":"The chain of infection has how many links, and which link do standard precautions such as hand hygiene attack?","choices":["Four links, and hand hygiene removes the reservoir","Six links, and hand hygiene breaks the mode of transmission","Six links, and hand hygiene protects the susceptible host","Eight links, and hand hygiene closes the portal of entry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The chain runs agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry and susceptible host, and hand hygiene is aimed squarely at the transmission link because it is the one most readily interrupted. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 6-14"},{"q":"What separates a vector from a reservoir, and what separates an active carrier from a passive one?","choices":["A vector is always an insect and a reservoir always an animal; both carriers are infected","The reservoir carries it and the vector harbours it; an active carrier shows symptoms, a passive one does not","The vector carries it and the reservoir harbours it; an active carrier is infected, a passive one is not","A vector is inanimate and a reservoir living; an active carrier is treated, a passive one is not"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A reservoir is where the organism normally lives and multiplies, a vector transports it to a host, and the passive carrier simply transfers organisms on the hands or clothing without being infected. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 16-26"},{"q":"Why can a zoonotic infection not be eradicated the way smallpox was?","choices":["Zoonotic organisms are resistant to all available antimicrobials","Zoonotic organisms mutate too quickly for any vaccine to remain effective","Zoonotic infections are always viral, and viruses cannot be eradicated","The animal reservoir persists even if every human case is cured"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Eradication requires removing every place the organism can live and multiply, and an infection maintained in animals continues regardless of what happens in the human population. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 28-36"},{"q":"A fomite transmits infection. What is a fomite, and why do hospital keyboards and stethoscopes matter so much?","choices":["An airborne droplet nucleus; these stay suspended near equipment","A living carrier that shows no symptoms; these move between patients unnoticed","A contaminated non-living object; these are touched constantly and cleaned rarely","A biofilm on a wet surface; these resist all disinfectants"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fomite is any inanimate object that carries organisms between people, and items handled many times a day but not included in routine cleaning become efficient relays. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 40-52"},{"q":"Which patients are most at risk from hospital-acquired infection, and which sites are most commonly involved?","choices":["The immunocompromised; urinary tract, lungs and surgical sites","The young and otherwise healthy; the skin and soft tissue","Patients on short admissions; the gastrointestinal tract alone","Outpatients attending clinics; the bloodstream alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Impaired defences allow organisms of low virulence to establish, and the commonest sites are exactly those breached by catheters, ventilation and surgery. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"What determines whether a disinfectant actually works on a surface?","choices":["The brand of the product and the material of the cloth","Its smell and the temperature of the room","Whether the surface looks clean before application","Its concentration and its contact time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An agent applied too dilute or wiped off before its contact time has elapsed will not achieve the reduction it is rated for, however effective the chemical is in principle. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 56-64"},{"q":"Bacteria acquire resistance genes from each other by horizontal gene transfer. Which mechanism uses a virus as the carrier, and which picks up free genetic material from the surroundings?","choices":["Transformation uses a phage; transduction takes up free material","Transduction uses a phage; transformation takes up free material","Conjugation uses a phage; transduction requires direct cell contact","Transduction requires direct cell contact; conjugation takes up free material"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In transduction a bacteriophage packages host genes and injects them into the next cell, whereas in transformation the recipient absorbs naked deoxyribonucleic acid released by a lysed neighbour. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 66-72"},{"q":"Of the three lines of defense, which is the specific one, and where does the real dividing line fall?","choices":["The third is specific; the line falls between the second and third","The first is specific; the line falls between the first and second","The second is specific; the line falls between the first and second","All three are specific to differing degrees, so no single line divides them"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The first two lines are both nonspecific, acting the same way against anything, and only the third is acquired through exposure to a particular antigen and carries memory of it. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Intact skin, lysozyme in tears and the resident flora each block invasion. Which category does each belong to?","choices":["Skin is physical, lysozyme chemical, resident flora microbiological","Skin is chemical, lysozyme physical, resident flora genetic","All three are physical barriers differing only in location","Skin is microbiological, lysozyme genetic, resident flora chemical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The first line divides into physical barriers that obstruct, chemical substances that attack, and microbiological competition from the normal flora that occupies the niche first. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 6-18"},{"q":"Lysozyme in tears attacks peptidoglycan. Reasoning forward, which organisms does it strip of their whole wall, and why does the tougher-looking one fare worse?","choices":["Gram-negative organisms, because their thin peptidoglycan is easier to digest","Gram-positive organisms, whose thick peptidoglycan lies exposed","Both equally, because peptidoglycan is chemically identical in each","Neither, because lysozyme acts only on fungal cell walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The intuition that a thicker wall must be sturdier is backwards here: the peptidoglycan is the enzyme's substrate, and in the Gram-positive organism it is the outermost layer with nothing shielding it. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 13-18"},{"q":"Host cells recognise trouble through pattern recognition receptors. What do those receptors bind, and what is the difference between the two kinds of signal?","choices":["Pathogen-associated and damage-associated molecular patterns","Specific antigens from microbes and antibodies from plasma cells","Complement fragments from serum and cytokines from leukocytes","Only microbial patterns, since host molecules are always ignored"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pathogen-associated patterns are conserved microbial structures we never make ourselves; damage-associated patterns are molecules released by our own injured cells, so one receptor system responds to both infection and tissue injury. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 20-30"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is the most numerous, and what is its job?","choices":["The monocyte, at roughly 55 to 90 per cent, and it releases histamine","The lymphocyte, at roughly 55 to 90 per cent, and it presents antigen to other cells","The neutrophil, at roughly 55 to 90 per cent, and it is the dedicated phagocytic killer","The eosinophil, at roughly 55 to 90 per cent, and it destroys bacteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils dominate the differential count and arrive first at an infection, where they engulf and destroy organisms directly. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-70"},{"q":"Eosinophils and basophils are both rare. What does each of them actually do?","choices":["Eosinophils release histamine; basophils defend against helminths","Eosinophils defend against parasites; basophils release mediators","Both present antigen to lymphocytes as their principal function","Both are the body's principal phagocytes against bacteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophil granules carry molecules toxic to organisms too large to phagocytose, particularly helminths, while basophils release mediators such as histamine that drive the inflammatory and allergic response. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-70"},{"q":"A macrophage and a neutrophil are both phagocytes. What does the macrophage do that the neutrophil does not?","choices":["It releases histamine to increase vascular permeability","It circulates in the bloodstream while the neutrophil stays in tissue","It kills organisms directly while the neutrophil only marks them","It presents antigen to lymphocytes and secretes cytokines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil kills and dies, whereas the macrophage additionally processes what it engulfs and displays it to lymphocytes, which is the bridge to the specific immune response. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-72"},{"q":"A white blood count with differential is ordered. What does it measure, and what can it tell you that a total count alone cannot?","choices":["It totals each type of white cell and shows whether they are in normal proportion","It measures only the total number of white cells, more precisely than a plain count","It measures antibody levels against specific organisms","It identifies which organism is causing the infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A total count says how many cells there are, while the differential says which kinds, so a normal total can still conceal a badly skewed proportion pointing to bacterial or viral infection. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 70-74"},{"q":"Inflammation has four classical signs. Name them, and say how the redness and heat actually come about.","choices":["Redness, heat, swelling and pain; the first two from vasoconstriction concentrating blood","Redness, heat, swelling and pain; the first two from vasodilation increasing blood flow to the area","Redness, heat, pus and fever; the first two from neutrophils releasing pigment","Swelling, pain, itching and scaling; the first two from mast cell histamine alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rubor, calor, tumor and dolor are the four, and widening the arterioles brings more warm blood into the injured region, which produces both the redness and the heat. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 34-40"},{"q":"What causes fever, why is it beneficial, and why is it dangerous?","choices":["Dehydration raises core temperature; it conserves fluid, but causes confusion","Bacterial endotoxin acts directly on the skin; it kills all pathogens outright, and carries no real risk","Cytokines reset the set point; it inhibits pathogens but high fever harms tissue","Histamine from mast cells; it increases blood flow, but causes swelling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Interleukin-1, interleukin-6 and tumour necrosis factor act systemically to raise the hypothalamic set point; the higher temperature slows microbial growth and accelerates host defence, yet sustained extremes denature host proteins and harm the patient. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Which kind of organism prompts interferon production, and what exactly does interferon do to it?","choices":["A virus, and interferon does not kill it — it prevents spread to surrounding cells","A virus, and interferon destroys the viral particles directly","A bacterium, and interferon lyses it through the membrane attack complex","A parasite, and interferon coats it so phagocytes can engulf it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Interferon acts on neighbouring uninfected cells, inducing an antiviral state so the infection cannot propagate; the already-infected cell is not rescued and the virus is not destroyed. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 48-52"},{"q":"Three pathways can switch complement on. In what order are they activated, and what do all three converge on?","choices":["Lectin, then classical, then alternative, all converging on forming C1","Classical, then lectin, then alternative, all converging on cleaving C3","Alternative, then lectin, then classical, all converging on cleaving C3","Alternative, then classical, then lectin, all converging on forming antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The alternative pathway fires immediately with no prior recognition needed, the lectin pathway follows, and the classical pathway comes last because it waits on antibody; the difference between them is only how they start, since all three cleave C3. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 42-46"},{"q":"Cleaving C3 produces two fragments. What does each one do, and what makes the larger fragment able to stick to a pathogen?","choices":["C3b attaches and marks the surface; C3a recruits phagocytes; a thioester bond is exposed","C3a attaches to the surface and C3b recruits phagocytes, through an exposed disulphide bond","Both fragments attach to the surface, held by hydrogen bonding","C3b lyses the pathogen directly and C3a neutralises its toxins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cleavage exposes the thioester bond on C3b, which reacts with a hydroxyl or amino group on the pathogen surface to fix complement there, while C3a diffuses away as a chemoattractant. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 42-45"},{"q":"Neutrophils arrive at an infection and phagocytose bacteria. What happens to them afterwards, and what does the patient see as a result?","choices":["They mature into macrophages and remain in the tissue permanently","They recycle their granules and return to the circulation, so no visible change occurs","They cannot replenish their granules, so they die, and the accumulated dead cells appear as pus","They release histamine and cause the swelling seen at the site"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each neutrophil has one load of granule contents and no means of making more, so it is spent after its work and the collected remains form the visible purulent material. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 76-78"},{"q":"Immunity can be natural or artificial and active or passive. Which combination describes a person who receives antibody across the placenta?","choices":["Natural active","Natural passive","Artificial passive","Artificial active"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nothing was engineered, so it is natural, and the recipient is given ready-made antibody rather than producing it, so it is passive and therefore temporary. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 6-12"},{"q":"An antibody monomer is built from how many chains, and which fragment binds the antigen?","choices":["Two chains, one heavy and one light, with the constant fragment binding antigen","Four chains, two heavy and two light, with the antigen-binding fragment at the variable ends","Four chains, all identical, with the stem binding antigen","Six chains, three heavy and three light, with the hinge binding antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two identical heavy and two identical light chains form the Y, and the variable regions at the tips of the arms make up the fragment that binds antigen. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 40-48"},{"q":"Which antibody class appears first in a new infection, which is the most abundant overall, and which guards mucosal surfaces?","choices":["Immunoglobulin M first, immunoglobulin G most abundant, immunoglobulin A at mucosal surfaces","Immunoglobulin G first, immunoglobulin M most abundant, immunoglobulin E at mucosal surfaces","Immunoglobulin A first, immunoglobulin E most abundant, immunoglobulin D at mucosal surfaces","Immunoglobulin E first, immunoglobulin A most abundant, immunoglobulin M at mucosal surfaces"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pentameric immunoglobulin M is produced first and is by far the largest, immunoglobulin G dominates the circulating pool and crosses the placenta, and immunoglobulin A is secreted onto mucous membranes. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 50-60"},{"q":"Clonal selection explains how the right antibody is produced. When are the receptor specificities generated?","choices":["Before any antigen has entered, so the antigen selects a clone that already exists","After the antigen arrives, which instructs the cell to build a matching receptor","Only in the bone marrow during an active infection","Continuously in the thymus in response to circulating antigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The enormous repertoire is generated during lymphocyte development by gene rearrangement, so an arriving antigen does not instruct anything; it simply finds and expands the clone whose receptor already fits. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 30-38"},{"q":"Class one and class two major histocompatibility complex molecules present antigen to different cells. Which presents to which?","choices":["Class one presents to cytotoxic T cells; class two presents to helper T cells","Class one presents to helper T cells; class two presents to cytotoxic T cells","Class one presents to B cells; class two presents to natural killer cells","Both present only to helper T cells, differing in the tissues that carry them"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class one is on nearly every nucleated cell and displays what is being made inside it to the cytotoxic subset, while class two is on antigen-presenting cells and displays engulfed material to helper cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 20-28"},{"q":"Antibody protects in several ways. Which describes opsonisation, and which describes neutralisation?","choices":["Opsonisation clumps organisms together; neutralisation activates complement","Opsonisation blocks attachment sites; neutralisation coats the organism for phagocytosis","Opsonisation coats the organism so phagocytes engulf it; neutralisation blocks its attachment sites","Opsonisation lyses the organism; neutralisation recruits neutrophils"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Coating marks the target and gives the phagocyte something to grip, while neutralisation works by occupying the structures a virus or toxin needs in order to bind host cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 62-70"},{"q":"A patient meets the same antigen a second time. How does the response differ from the first, and what is it called?","choices":["It is weaker, because tolerance develops after the first exposure","It is slower but produces a higher titre of immunoglobulin M; it is the primary response","It is identical to the first, since memory affects only cell-mediated immunity","It is faster and produces a much higher titre of immunoglobulin G; it is the anamnestic response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells laid down by the first exposure are already present and pre-committed, so the second response begins sooner, climbs higher and is dominated by immunoglobulin G rather than immunoglobulin M. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 72-80"},{"q":"Which cell links the innate response to the specific one, and how does it do it?","choices":["The plasma cell, by detecting patterns on the pathogen surface","The neutrophil, by secreting antibody once it has phagocytosed an organism","The natural killer cell, by producing memory against the organism it destroys","The antigen-presenting cell, by displaying processed antigen on class two molecules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages and dendritic cells act within the nonspecific response but then present processed antigen to helper T cells, which is the step that recruits the specific system. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 6-18"}]};
